        {
          "fact": "L12",
          "source": "def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the affine Minkowski space with tangent spaces canonically identified with $V$ ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], [[lem-tangent-space-of-affine-minkowski-space-is-v]]), and let $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ be a nonempty open subset. All tensor fields below are $C^k$ fields in the sense of [[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]], with $k=\\infty$ unless a clause says otherwise.\n\n**(a) Vector fields.** For smooth vector fields $X,Y$ on $\\Omega$ define the **covariant derivative of $Y$ in the direction $X$** at $p\\in\\Omega$ by\n\n$$\\bigl(\\nabla_XY\\bigr)(p):=\\frac{d}{dt}\\Big|_{t=0}Y\\bigl(p+tX(p)\\bigr),$$\n\nthe derivative of the $V$-valued composite $t\\mapsto Y(p+tX(p))$, which is defined for $t$ in a neighbourhood of $0$ because $\\Omega$ is open ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-derivative]]). In the inertial chart $x$ of any inertial frame one has $(\\nabla_XY)^a=X^b\\,\\partial_bY^a$; the value does not depend on the inertial chart: for another inertial chart $x'=\\Lambda x+b$ one has $\\partial'_a=(\\Lambda^{-1})^b_{\\ a}\\,\\partial_b$ and $Y'^a=\\Lambda^a_{\\ b}Y^b$ ([[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]], [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]), and $\\Lambda$ is constant, so $(\\Lambda^{-1})^a_{\\ c}\\,X'^b\\,\\partial'_bY'^c=X^b\\,\\partial_bY^a$. The field $\\nabla_XY$ is again a smooth vector field on $\\Omega$.\n\n**(b) Covector and general tensor fields.** For a smooth covector field $\\alpha$ set $(\\nabla_X\\alpha)(Y):=X(\\alpha(Y))-\\alpha(\\nabla_XY)$ for every smooth vector field $Y$, and for a type $(r,s)$ tensor field $T$ define $\\nabla_XT$ by the derivation rule on the evaluation of $T$: for $T:(V^*)^r\\times V^s\\to\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$(\\nabla_XT)(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_r;Y_1,\\dots,Y_s)=X\\bigl[T(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_r;Y_1,\\dots,Y_s)\\bigr]-\\sum_{i=1}^rT(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\nabla_X\\alpha_i,\\dots,\\alpha_r;Y_1,\\dots,Y_s)-\\sum_{j=1}^sT(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_r;Y_1,\\dots,\\nabla_XY_j,\\dots,Y_s),$$\n\nwhere the vector and covector arguments are smooth fields on $\\Omega$. In inertial coordinates every component of $\\nabla_XT$ is $X^b\\,\\partial_b$ applied to that component, with no connection coefficients.\n\n**(c) Properties.** The operator $\\nabla$ is $\\mathbb R$-bilinear, is $C^\\infty(\\Omega)$-linear in $X$, and satisfies the Leibniz rules $\\nabla_X(fY)=X(f)Y+f\\nabla_XY$ and the analogous rules for tensor fields; it is metric compatible, $\\nabla\\eta=0$, because $\\partial_a\\eta_{bc}=0$ in every inertial chart; it is torsion-free, $\\nabla_XY-\\nabla_YX=[X,Y]$, by the coordinate formula for the Lie bracket ([[def-lie-bracket-of-smooth-vector-fields]], [[prop-coordinate-formula-for-the-lie-bracket]]); and it has zero curvature, $\\nabla_X\\nabla_YZ-\\nabla_Y\\nabla_XZ-\\nabla_{[X,Y]}Z=0$, by equality of mixed partial derivatives of the components ([[thm-young-mixed-partial-theorem]]).\n\n**(d) Fields along a curve.** If $\\gamma:I\\to A$ is a $C^1$ curve and $W:I\\to V$ is a $C^1$ field along it ([[def-vector-field-and-section-along-a-smooth-curve]]), the **covariant derivative of $W$ along $\\gamma$** is the ordinary $V$-valued derivative $DW/ds:=W'$ ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]), because the canonical identification $T_pA=V$ is independent of $p$; consequently parallel transport along any curve is the identity and a field is parallel exactly when it is constant. This derivative is the one used for the acceleration of congruences, for Fermi–Walker transport and for the kinematic decomposition below; the model needs no Christoffel symbols and no parallel-transport ODE in this formulation.\n\n**(e) First-order $C^1$ version.** For $C^1$ vector fields $X,Y$ and $C^1$ covector or tensor fields on $\\Omega$ the same directional and evaluation definitions are well-defined and have continuous coefficients: in an inertial chart $(\\nabla_XY)^a=X^b\\partial_bY^a$, and each tensor component is differentiated in the same way. The component recovery and the subtraction of all argument derivatives remain valid; $C^1$ scalar multiplication satisfies the same first-order Leibniz rules; metric compatibility remains $\\nabla\\eta=0$; and the first-order torsion identity uses the continuous coordinate bracket $[X,Y]^a=X^b\\partial_bY^a-Y^b\\partial_bX^a$. No $C^1$ closure of this bracket and no second covariant derivative of arbitrary $C^1$ data are asserted. For $C^2$ vector fields the second derivatives needed for the zero-curvature identity of (c) exist, and that identity follows by equality of the continuous mixed second partials. All smooth-field properties above and the $C^1$ along-curve derivative in (d) are retained.",
