          "source": "def-fixed-support-test-function-frechet-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an integer $n\\ge1$, a compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega$, and an open set $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, put $\\mathcal D_K=\\{\\varphi\\in\\mathcal D(\\Omega):\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi\\subseteq K\\}$, using [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]. For integers $m\\ge0$ set\n\n$$p_m(\\varphi)=\\max_{|\\alpha|\\le m}\\sup_{x\\in K}|\\partial^\\alpha\\varphi(x)|,\\qquad d(\\varphi,\\psi)=\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty2^{-m-1}\\min(1,p_m(\\varphi-\\psi)).$$\n\nThe topology is generated by the seminorms $p_m$: neighborhoods at zero contain finite intersections of $p_m<\\varepsilon$ with $\\varepsilon>0$. Derivatives are continuous on $K$, so these suprema are finite; for $K=\\varnothing$ all suprema are defined as zero and $\\mathcal D_K=\\{0\\}$. For nonempty $K$, $p_0$ separates functions because all functions vanish off $K$. These are increasing seminorms and the displayed series converges since its terms are at most $2^{-m-1}$.\n\nThe term **fixed-support Fr\u00e9chet space** refers to this topology and metric. Their agreement and completeness are established by the registered justifier [[lem-fixed-support-test-function-spaces-are-complete]], rather than included as unproved consequences of the name. If $K$ has empty interior, every test supported in $K$ is zero: a nonzero continuous value would have a neighborhood of nonzero values inside $K$.",
