    "lem-em-moving-flux": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-em-moving-patch",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ be a nonempty finite elementary Green region in the sense of [[def-type-i-type-ii-and-elementary-green-regions]], written as $D=D_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup D_N$ with its supplied decomposition into elementary Green regions of pairwise disjoint interiors and with the shared-arc structure of that definition, and let $\\partial D$ be its positive boundary chain ([[def-positive-orientation-for-elementary-region-boundaries]]). We require in addition that $D$ be Jordan measurable; this is explained below and holds for every finite elementary Green region. Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval and let\n\n$$r:D\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$$\n\nbe a map that extends to a $C^2$ map ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) on an open neighbourhood of $D\\times I\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$. Write $p=(u,v)$ for the parameter point, write $r_u(p,t),r_v(p,t),r_t(p,t)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ for the partial derivatives of that $C^2$ extension at $(p,t)$, and require\n\n$$r_u(p,t)\\times r_v(p,t)\\ne0\\qquad\\text{for every }(p,t)\\in D^\\circ\\times I^\\circ,$$\n\nthe cross product being that of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. For every $t\\in I^\\circ$ the slice $r(\\cdot,\\cdot,t)$ is therefore a regular parametrized patch over $D$ in the sense of [[def-admissible-regular-parametrized-surface-patch]]; at an endpoint of a nondegenerate interval $I$, and when $I$ is a singleton, the condition is vacuous and no regularity of that slice is asserted.\n\nThe **swept set** of the family is\n\n$$S:=\\bigcup_{t\\in I}r(D\\times\\{t\\})\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3 .$$\n\nA **velocity field for $r$** is a pair $(W,v)$ consisting of an open set $W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ with $S\\subseteq W$ and a map $v:W\\to\\mathbb R^3$ of class $C^1$ such that\n\n$$v\\bigl(r(p,t),t\\bigr)=r_t(p,t)\\qquad\\text{for every }(p,t)\\in D\\times I .$$\n\nSuch a pair is required to exist: the standing hypothesis of this definition is that a velocity field for $r$ is given, and $v$ denotes it. The equality above is what single-valuedness means here: whenever two parameter points map to the same spatial point, the corresponding time derivatives agree, so that $v$ is a well-defined function on the swept set.\n\nFinally let $B:V\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be a $C^1$ field on an open set $V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ containing $S$. The **flux of $B$ through the patch at time $t\\in I$** is\n\n$$\\Phi(t):=\\int_D\\bigl\\langle B\\bigl(r(p,t),t\\bigr),\\,r_u(p,t)\\times r_v(p,t)\\bigr\\rangle\\,du\\,dv,$$\n\nthe integral being the Riemann integral over the compact Jordan measurable set $D$ of the displayed continuous integrand, and the pairing being the Euclidean inner product of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. For fixed $t$, $\\Phi(t)$ is the flux of $B(\\cdot,t)$ through the regular patch $(D,r(\\cdot,\\cdot,t))$ in the orientation induced by the parametrization ([[def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch]]), with $r_u\\times r_v$ the oriented area vector in place of the unit normal.\n\nIf the family is intended to describe an actual material sheet, one requires in addition that $r(\\cdot,\\cdot,t):D\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be injective for every $t\\in I$, so that each spatial slice is an embedded sheet; this condition is not part of the standing hypotheses and is not used by the flux formula above.\n\n### Well-definedness and conventions\n\n*Jordan measurability of $D$.* Each piece $D_i$ is compact and Jordan measurable ([[def-type-i-type-ii-and-elementary-green-regions]]), so its boundary has content zero ([[thm-jordan-boundary-criterion]]); the boundary $\\partial D$ is contained in the finite union of the $\\partial D_i$ together with the shared arcs and endpoints, a null set ([[lem-null-sets-in-rn-closed-under-subsets-and-countable-unions]]); being a compact null set, this union has content zero ([[lem-compact-null-iff-content-zero-in-rn]]), so $\\partial D$ has content zero and the bounded set $D$ is Jordan measurable ([[thm-jordan-boundary-criterion]]). Finite additivity of Jordan content over the pieces is the statement of [[cor-jordan-content-finite-additivity]].\n\n*Extension independence of the spatial partials, and of $r_t$ for nondegenerate $I$.* If $r$ has two $C^2$ extensions $\\tilde r_1,\\tilde r_2$ to open neighbourhoods of $D\\times I$, then they agree on $D\\times I$. For the spatial partials, fix $(p,t)\\in D\\times I$: if $p\\in D^\\circ$ then for small $h$ the points $(p+h e_u,t)$ and $(p+h e_v,t)$ lie in $D\\times I$, so the difference quotients agree and $\\partial_u\\tilde r_1(p,t)=\\partial_u\\tilde r_2(p,t)$, likewise for $\\partial_v$; if $p\\in\\partial D$, choose $p_n\\in D^\\circ$ with $p_n\\to p$, observe $\\partial_u\\tilde r_1(p_n,t)=\\partial_u\\tilde r_2(p_n,t)$ for every $n$, and pass to the limit using continuity of both first partials on a common neighbourhood. Hence $r_u$ and $r_v$ on $D\\times I$ are determined by $r$ alone in every case. For $r_t$, the same limit argument with points $(p_n,t_n)$, $p_n\\in D^\\circ$, $t_n\\in I^\\circ$, $t_n\\to t$, determines $r_t$ by $r$ alone whenever $t$ is a limit of interior points of $I$, that is, whenever $I$ is nondegenerate. If $I=\\{t_0\\}$ is a singleton, then $I^\\circ=\\varnothing$ and $r_t$ may genuinely depend on the extension: the zero map on $D\\times\\{t_0\\}$ has the two $C^2$ extensions $0$ and $(t-t_0)\\chi(p)$ with $\\chi$ any smooth function, whose time derivatives at $t_0$ differ. The integrand below involves only $r_u\\times r_v$, so it is unambiguous in every case.\n\n*Integrability.* For fixed $t\\in I$, the map $p\\mapsto B(r(p,t),t)$ is continuous on $D$ as a composition of continuous maps, the maps $p\\mapsto r_u(p,t)$ and $p\\mapsto r_v(p,t)$ are continuous on $D$, and the cross product and inner product are continuous bilinear operations; hence the integrand is continuous on the compact set $D$. A continuous real function on a compact Jordan measurable set is Riemann integrable over it ([[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]]), so $\\Phi(t)$ is defined for every $t\\in I$.\n\n*Degenerate time domains.* If $I$ is empty then $D\\times I$ is empty, the family is vacuous and no flux value is asserted. If $I=\\{t_0\\}$ is a singleton, the compatibility condition still reads $v(r(p,t_0),t_0)=r_t(p,t_0)$, but it can always be met: choosing the extension $\\tilde r(p,t):=r(p,t_0)$, which is constant in the time variable, gives $r_t\\equiv0$ and admits the compatible $C^1$ velocity field $v\\equiv0$. With a different extension the compatible velocity field is correspondingly different; as explained above, $r_t(p,t_0)$ is not determined by $r$ alone in this case. The flux $\\Phi(t_0)$ involves only $r_u\\times r_v$, so it is the flux of the fixed patch $(D,r(\\cdot,\\cdot,t_0))$ and does not depend on that choice. The condition $r_u\\times r_v\\ne0$ is imposed only on $D^\\circ\\times I^\\circ$, so rank failures on the boundary of the parameter region (seams) are permitted exactly as in [[def-admissible-regular-parametrized-surface-patch]], and at an endpoint time of a nondegenerate $I$ the definition asserts no regularity of the slice.\n\n*No physical assertion.* This is a definition of a mathematical object: a regular time-dependent parametrized patch, a velocity field compatible with it, and the flux integral. It asserts nothing about electromagnetic fields, about which vector fields occur in nature, or about the derivative of $\\Phi$; the transport identity for $\\Phi'$ is proved separately.",
