          "claim": "The magnetic field and regularity. By [F15] and the chain rule, $\\operatorname{curl}A=\\nabla(\\varphi/c)\\times\\beta+(\\varphi/c)\\operatorname{curl}\\beta$ with $\\operatorname{curl}\\beta=\\nabla s\\times\\dot\\beta$, so $\\operatorname{curl}A=-\\frac{C\\nabla D\\times\\beta}{cD^2}-\\frac{C\\,n\\times\\dot\\beta}{c^2\\kappa^2r}$. Substituting $\\nabla D$ from step 1.4 and using $n\\times(n-\\beta)=-n\\times\\beta$ gives $\\operatorname{curl}A=-C\\bigl[\\frac{1-|\\beta|^2+rd/c}{c\\kappa^3r^2}(n\\times\\beta)+\\frac{n\\times\\dot\\beta}{c^2\\kappa^2r}\\bigr]$. Crossing the field of step 4.1 with $n/c$ gives the same expression, because $n\\times n=0$, $n\\times(n-\\beta)=-n\\times\\beta$ and $n\\times(d(n-\\beta)-\\kappa\\dot\\beta)=-d(n\\times\\beta)-\\kappa(n\\times\\dot\\beta)$; hence $B=n\\times E/c$. Since for $z\\in C^3$ the root is $C^3$ and $\\nabla D,D_t,\\beta,\\dot\\beta,\\kappa,r,n$ are as in step 1.4 with $\\kappa r>0$ on $V$, both displayed fields are $C^1$ on $V$; the same computation with a $C^k$ trajectory uses the $C^k$ root branch of [F2] and gives $C^{k-2}$ fields, and the smooth branch gives smooth fields. \u220e",
