     "quote": "Let $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ be the laboratory region of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the units, constants and symbols of [[def-em-si-conventions]], let $E:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and $B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be fields of [[def-em-fields]] and let $(\\rho,J)$ be source fields of [[def-em-sources]] on $\\Omega\\times I$, all of class $C^1$ (with every additional derivative appearing below existing and continuous); $\\partial_t$ is the partial time derivative at fixed position of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]. (a) [Static regime] The **static (time-independent) electromagnetic regime** on $\\Omega\\times I$ is the requirement $$\\partial_tE=0,\\qquad\\partial_tB=0,\\qquad\\partial_t\\rho=0,\\qquad\\partial_tJ=0\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega\\times I.$$ (b) [Pure electrostatics] The **pure electrostatic regime** is the static regime together with $J=0$ and $B=0$ on $\\Omega\\times I$. (c) [Reduced equations] If the smooth branch of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] holds in the static regime, then the four Maxwell equations reduce to $$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad\\operatorname{curl}E=0,\\qquad\\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J$$ pointwise on $\\Omega\\times I$, with $\\operatorname{div}$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] applied at each fixed time; in the pure electrostatic regime the reduction is exactly the pair $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$",
