     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set, and $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]], [[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so $$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$ is the function space of [[def-function-space]] at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$, a vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations ([[def-vector-space]]). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the same set that [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$. The **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number $$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$ the finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]] applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$ (extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is)",
