     "quote": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be an oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]), let $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an inertial frame with inertial chart $x:A\\to\\mathbb R^4$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]), let $c>0$ be the framework's conversion constant, and let $\\delta$ be the Euclidean spatial form of that frame on $n^\\perp$, $n:=e_0$, with norm $|\\cdot|$ and positive spatial orientation ([[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]). Fix a nonempty open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ ([[def-interval]]) and a nonempty open set $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$, and put $$U:=\\{p\\in A:\\ \\text{there are }t\\in I,\\ y\\in\\Omega\\text{ with }x^0(p)=ct,\\ (x^1,x^2,x^3)(p)=y\\}.$$ Then: (a) [Chart restriction] $x(U)=\\{(ct,y):t\\in I,\\ y\\in\\Omega\\}$ and $\\Phi:U\\to I\\times\\Omega$, $\\Phi(p):=(x^0(p)/c,(x^1,x^2,x^3)(p))$, is a bijection, because the inertial chart $x:A\\to\\mathbb R^4$ is a bijection and $c>0$; its components are the **laboratory time coordinate** $t(p):=x^0(p)/c$ and the **laboratory position** $r(p):=(x^1(p),x^2(p),x^3(p))$. In the chart coordinates the pair $(t(p),r(p))$ is the reading of the laboratory decomposition of $p$. Writing $r(p)=(r^1(p),r^2(p),r^3(p))\\in\\mathbb R^3$, the standard basis of $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]) labels the three coordinate directions, and the identification $\\mathbb R^3\\cong n^\\perp$ is the linear map $\\sum_{i=1}^3 r^i e_i$ into the three-dimensional real vector space $n^\\perp$ ([[def-vector-space]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). (b) [Slices and spatial structure] For each $t\\in I$ the slice $U_t:=\\{p\\in U:t(p)=t\\}$ is carried bijectively onto $\\Omega$ by $r$; the constant vector $n=e_0$ is future unit timelike, and $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ is the $\\delta$-orthonormal positively oriented basis of $n^\\perp$ in which the coordinates of $r$ are expressed",
