     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "For the Lebesgue branches (a) and (b) assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from the exact measure interface ([[def-countable-choice]]); the finite sums in branch (c) are defined and computed without Choice. Let the laboratory region, its chart, its spatial Euclidean structure and its units be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]], and let $\\delta_{ij}$ be the Kronecker symbol in the $\\delta$-orthonormal basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of the frame, with $|y|$ the Euclidean norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. This definition assigns **source moments** to a static charge distribution in the fixed-time reading of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (d) and (e). Three branches are declared. (a) [Absolutely continuous branch] Let $a>0$ and let $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ be real ([[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]) and vanish $\\lambda_3$-almost everywhere outside the closed ball $\\overline B_a(0)=\\{y:|y|\\le a\\}$ ([[def-metric-ball]], [[def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere]]). The **total charge** $Q$, the **dipole moment** $p$, the **degree-$l$ Cartesian moments** $M_{i_1\\dots i_l}$ and the **traceless quadrupole** $Q_{ij}$ are $$Q:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3,\\qquad p_i:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}y_i\\,\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y),\\qquad M_{i_1\\dots i_l}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}y_{i_1}\\cdots y_{i_l}\\,\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\ (l\\ge1),$$ $$M^{(0)}:=Q,\\qquad M_i:=p_i,\\qquad Q_{ij}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\bigl(3y_iy_j-|y|^2\\delta_{ij}\\bigr)\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y),$$ with $l\\ge1$ and indices in $\\{1,2,3\\}$; units are C for $Q$, C m for $p$, C m$^l$ for the degree-$l$ Cartesian moment and C m$^2$ for $Q_{ij}$, and all integrals are in the sense of [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]] and [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]. Each integral is absolutely convergent: since $\\rho=0$ $\\lambda_3$-a.e. off $\\overline B_a(0)$, and the maps $y\\mapsto y_{i_1}\\cdots y_{i_l}$ and $y\\mapsto 3y_iy_j-|y|^2\\delta_{ij}$ are bounded $\\lambda_3$-a.e. on that ball by $a^l$ and by $4a^2$ respectively, the integrands are dominated $\\lambda_3$-a.e. by $a^l|\\rho|$ and $4a^2|\\rho|$, which lie in $L^1$ ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). The traceless quadrupole is traceless, $$\\sum_{i=1}^3Q_{ii}=0,$$ because $\\sum_{i=1}^3(3y_i^2-|y|^2)=0$ pointwise, and summing the three integrable functions $3y_i^2\\rho-|y|^2\\rho$ and applying finite linearity of the integral on $L^1$ ([[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]]) evaluates the sum as the integral of the pointwise-zero function; replacing $\\rho$ by an equal $\\lambda_3$-a.e. density changes no moment ([[thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality]]). (b) [General locally integrable branch] If instead $\\rho\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^3)$ is real and $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|y|^l|\\rho(y)|\\,d\\lambda_3(y)<\\infty$ for a given integer $l\\ge0$, then the degree-$l$ Cartesian moments of the same formula are absolutely convergent and defined; if the absolute weighted moment $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|y|^L|\\rho(y)|\\,d\\lambda_3(y)$ is finite for some real $L\\ge0$, then every integer Cartesian degree $0\\le l\\le L$ is defined, and in particular $Q=\\int\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3$ is absolutely convergent, so that $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ is a conclusion of this branch and not an added hypothesis. Here $\\rho$ is restricted to the closed unit ball, where it is integrable by local integrability, and outside it $|y|^l\\le|y|^L$; splitting the nonnegative integral over these two measurable sets and applying monotonicity and additivity of the integral gives $\\int|y|^l|\\rho|\\le\\int_{\\overline B_1(0)}|\\rho|+\\int_{\\{|y|>1\\}}|y|^L|\\rho|<\\infty$ ([[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]], [[def-metric-ball]]). The convention $|y|^0=1$ includes the origin, no compact support and no $L^1$-membership is assumed in this branch, and Cartesian monomials of degree $l$ have modulus at most $|y|^l$, so all lower moments, including $Q$, exist together with the degree-$l$ moment. (c) [Finite system of stationary point charges] Let $N\\ge0$, let $x_1,\\dots,x_N\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and let $q_1,\\dots,q_N\\in\\mathbb R$ be charge parameters ([[def-em-sources]] (a)), read as the stationary point charges $q_k\\delta_{x_k}$ in the fixed-time reading of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e); for $N=0$ the lists are empty and every sum below is the empty sum $0$. Define $$Q:=\\sum_{k=1}^Nq_k,\\qquad p:=\\sum_{k=1}^Nq_kx_k,\\qquad M_{i_1\\dots i_l}:=\\sum_{k=1}^Nq_k\\,x_{k,i_1}\\cdots x_{k,i_l}\\ (l\\ge1),\\qquad M^{(0)}:=Q,$$ $$Q_{ij}:=\\sum_{k=1}^Nq_k\\bigl(3x_{k,i}x_{k,j}-|x_k|^2\\delta_{ij}\\bigr),$$ finite sums in the sense of [[def-finite-sum]] ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]]); again $$\\sum_{i=1}^3Q_{ii}=0,$$ by finite distributivity",
