     "source": "def-em-electrostatic-regime",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "Let $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ be the laboratory region of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the units, constants and symbols of [[def-em-si-conventions]], let $E:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and $B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be fields of [[def-em-fields]] and let $(\\rho,J)$ be source fields of [[def-em-sources]] on $\\Omega\\times I$, all of class $C^1$ (with every additional derivative appearing below existing and continuous); $\\partial_t$ is the partial time derivative at fixed position of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]. (a) [Static regime] The **static (time-independent) electromagnetic regime** on $\\Omega\\times I$ is the requirement $$\\partial_tE=0,\\qquad\\partial_tB=0,\\qquad\\partial_t\\rho=0,\\qquad\\partial_tJ=0\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega\\times I.$$ (b) [Pure electrostatics] The **pure electrostatic regime** is the static regime together with $J=0$ and $B=0$ on $\\Omega\\times I$. (c) [Reduced equations] If the smooth branch of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] holds in the static regime, then the four Maxwell equations reduce to $$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad\\operatorname{curl}E=0,\\qquad\\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J$$ pointwise on $\\Omega\\times I$, with $\\operatorname{div}$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] applied at each fixed time; in the pure electrostatic regime the reduction is exactly the pair $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$. (d) [Fixed-time reading] In the static regime $E,B,\\rho,J$ do not depend on $t$, so each is the pullback along $(y,t)\\mapsto y$ of a single map on $\\Omega$; this fixed-time reading (the spatial field or source) is the object analysed by the electrostatic statements of this page, and no other time dependence is introduced. The equations of (c) are equations between those spatial maps. (e) [Static-to-distributional reading] Let fields $E,B$ and volume densities $\\rho,J$ be locally integrable and time independent on $\\Omega\\times I$, and let $\\Omega$ be open. Then, under the $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention of the cited measure and distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]), the weak equations of the solution predicate of [[def-em-weak-sources]](e) for these time-independent objects hold if and only if the four spatial equations $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$, $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J$ hold in $\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$; the passage between them is the integration in $t$ of the pairings against $C_c^\\infty(I\\times\\Omega)$ tests, justified by [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] after identifying fields and densities with their regular distributions ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]). In particular a **stationary point charge** at $x_0\\in\\Omega$ with charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ is the point source of [[def-em-weak-sources]](b) with constant trajectory $z(t)\\equiv x_0$, whose pairing is $\\psi\\mapsto q\\int_I\\psi(x_0,t)\\,dt$ and whose fixed-time reading is the spatial point source $\\eta\\mapsto q\\eta(x_0)$ ([[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]])",
