     "source_section": "Statement",
     "quote": "For the density-integral branch assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) through the Lebesgue interface ([[def-countable-choice]]); the finite-point branch of all identities below is choice-free. Let $a>0$ and let $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ be real and vanish $\\lambda_3$-a.e. outside $\\overline B_a(0)$ ([[def-metric-ball]]), or let $(x_k,q_k)_{k=1}^N$ be a finite system of stationary point charges, with the source moments of [[def-em-electric-moments]] branches (a) and (c) respectively. For $b\\in\\mathbb R^3$ define the moments **about the reference point $b$** by replacing, in each integrand of [[def-em-electric-moments]] (a) and in each summand of (c), the vector $y$ by $y-b$ (respectively $x_k$ by $x_k-b$): $$Q^{(b)}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3,\\qquad p^{(b)}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}(y-b)\\,\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y),$$ $$M^{(b)}_{i_1\\dots i_l}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\prod_{r=1}^l(y-b)_{i_r}\\,\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\quad(l\\ge1),\\qquad M^{(b,0)}:=Q^{(b)},$$ $$Q^{(b)}_{ij}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\bigl(3(y-b)_i(y-b)_j-|y-b|^2\\delta_{ij}\\bigr)\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y),$$ and analogously with the finite sums $M^{(b)}_{i_1\\dots i_l}=\\sum_{k=1}^Nq_k\\prod_{r=1}^l(x_k-b)_{i_r}$ $(l\\ge1)$ and $Q^{(b)}_{ij}=\\sum_{k=1}^Nq_k\\bigl(3(x_k-b)_i(x_k-b)_j-|x_k-b|^2\\delta_{ij}\\bigr)$ in the point-charge branch; for $N=0$ the sums are the empty sum $0$. Every displayed integral is absolutely convergent: since $\\rho=0$ $\\lambda_3$-a.e. off $\\overline B_a(0)$, the estimates $|(y-b)_{i_1}\\cdots(y-b)_{i_l}|\\le(|y|+|b|)^l\\le(a+|b|)^l$ and $|3(y-b)_i(y-b)_j-|y-b|^2\\delta_{ij}|\\le4(|y|+|b|)^2\\le4(a+|b|)^2$ hold $\\lambda_3$-a.e. on the support, so each integrand is dominated $\\lambda_3$-a.e. by a finite constant times $|\\rho|\\in L^1$ ([[def-em-electric-moments]] (a), [[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Then: (a) $Q^{(b)}=Q$ for every $b$; (b) $p^{(b)}=p-Qb$, so the dipole moment is independent of the reference point exactly when $Q=0$; (c) for every $l\\ge0$, $$M^{(b)}_{i_1\\dots i_l}=\\sum_{S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,l\\}}(-1)^{|S|}\\Bigl(\\prod_{r\\in S}b_{i_r}\\Bigr)M_{S^{c}},$$ where for a subset $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,l\\}$ we write $S^{c}:=\\{1,\\dots,l\\}\\setminus S$ and $M_{S^{c}}$ for the Cartesian moment whose index list is $(i_r)_{r\\in S^{c}}$ in increasing order of $r$, with the empty index list read as $M_{\\emptyset}:=Q$; in particular $$M^{(b)}_{ij}=M_{ij}-b_ip_j-b_jp_i+b_ib_jQ;$$ (d) $Q^{(b)}_{ij}=Q_{ij}-3\\bigl(b_ip_j+b_jp_i\\bigr)+2(b\\cdot p)\\delta_{ij}+\\bigl(3b_ib_j-|b|^2\\delta_{ij}\\bigr)Q$; consequently, if $Q=0$ and $p=0$, then $Q^{(b)}_{ij}=Q_{ij}$ for every $b$; (e) [Scope] this is an exact identity between the declared source moments of [[def-em-electric-moments]]; it asserts nothing about fields, forces, dynamics or nature, and the reference point is a choice of coordinate origin, not an operation performed on the source",
