      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-newtonian-potential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and $n\\ge2$. For a measurable compactly supported\n$f$, define\n$$Nf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\\Phi(x-y)f(y)\\,dy$$\nat each $x$ where the integral is absolutely finite. Whenever this integral\nis finite almost everywhere, $Nf$ also denotes its almost-everywhere class.\nThe following items establish everywhere convergence for bounded compact data\nand almost-everywhere convergence for compact $L^1$ data; the definition itself\nmakes no unconditional convergence claim.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and $n\\ge2$. With $\\omega_{n-1}=|S^{n-1}|>0$ in the published chart/polar convention, set $\\Phi(x)=|x|^{2-n}/((n-2)\\omega_{n-1})$ for $n\\ge3$ and $\\Phi(x)=-(2\\pi)^{-1}\\log|x|$ for $n=2$, $x\\ne0$. Extend it as a locally integrable function at the pole. The one-dimensional analogue is $-\\tfrac12|x|$; the subsequent $n\\ge2$ Green theory does not silently include it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and $n\\ge2$. The chart surface measure on $S^{n-1}$ equals the polar measure $\\sigma$. Orthogonal transformations preserve it. The map $\\omega\\mapsto a+R\\omega$, $R>0$, multiplies surface measure by $R^{n-1}$, and $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\ge1$, $V_n(1)=\\pi^{n/2}/\\Gamma(n/2+1)$. Here $n$ is an integer.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-real-gamma-functional-equation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $s>0$, $\\Gamma(s+1)=s\\Gamma(s)$, and $\\Gamma(1)=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-real-gamma-one-half-is-root-pi",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$\\Gamma(1/2)=\\sqrt\\pi$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-laplace-fundamental-kernel-is-locally-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and $n\\ge2$. The normalized Laplace kernel $\\Phi$ is\nlocally integrable on $\\mathbb R^n$: its singularity is $\\log|x|$ for $n=2$\nand $|x|^{2-n}$ for $n\\ge3$. It therefore defines a regular distribution.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-support-and-compactly-supported-riemann-integral-in-rn",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$. For $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$, its **support** is\n$$\\operatorname{supp}f:=\\overline{\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^n:f(x)\\ne0\\}}.$$\nThe function is **compactly supported** if $\\operatorname{supp}f$ is compact.\n\nA compactly supported $f$ is **compactly supported Riemann integrable** if there is a nondegenerate closed rectangle $Q$ with $\\operatorname{supp}f\\subseteq\\operatorname{int}Q$ such that $f|_Q$ is Riemann integrable. Its integral over Euclidean space is defined by\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f:=\\int_Qf.$$\nThe value is independent of $Q$ by [[lem-compactly-supported-riemann-integral-is-well-defined]]. When the support is empty, $f=0$ and the value is $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $k\\in\\mathbb N$, let $m,q\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$. A map $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is of class $C^k$ when each component is of class $C^k$. Each scalar component uses the word-derivative convention of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]], whose source dimension is positive. The map is **smooth**, or $C^\\infty$, when it is $C^k$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nLet $n\\ge1$ and let $U,V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open. A bijection $f:U\\to V$ is a **$C^k$ diffeomorphism** when both $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are $C^k$. For $k\\ge1$, a **local $C^k$ diffeomorphism at $a\\in U$** is a restriction $f|_P:P\\to Q$ between open neighbourhoods of $a$ and $f(a)$ that is a $C^k$ diffeomorphism. At $k=1$ this agrees with [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]: continuous first partial derivatives give the required total derivative by [[thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability]], while continuity of the total derivative gives continuity of its matrix entries and hence of the first partial derivatives.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$. A **multi-index** is $\\alpha=(\\alpha_0,\\ldots,\\alpha_{m-1})\\in\\mathbb N^m$. Put\n\n$$|\\alpha|:=\\sum_{i<m}\\alpha_i,\\qquad \\alpha!:=\\prod_{i<m}\\alpha_i!,\\qquad h^\\alpha:=\\prod_{i<m}h_i^{\\alpha_i}\\quad(h\\in\\mathbb R^m).$$\n\nHere $|\\alpha|$ and $\\alpha!$ use the natural-number sum and product of [[def-nat-finite-sum-and-product]], and $n!$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]]. By contrast, $h^\\alpha$ is the finite product in $\\mathbb R$ of [[def-finite-sum]], with the natural exponents interpreted by [[def-integer-power]]. For the zero multi-index $0$, set $D^0f:=f$. For nonzero $\\alpha$, write\n\n$$D^\\alpha f:=\\partial_0^{\\alpha_0}\\cdots\\partial_{m-1}^{\\alpha_{m-1}}f$$\n\nfor this displayed, canonical order whenever it exists. Coordinate partial derivatives have the meaning fixed in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\nFor $k\\in\\mathbb N$, $f$ is of class $C^k$ on $U$ when, for every word $(i_1,\\ldots,i_r)$ of coordinate indices with $0\\le r\\le k$, the iterated derivative $\\partial_{i_r}\\cdots\\partial_{i_1}f$ exists and is continuous on $U$; the word of length $0$ denotes $f$. Thus this definition does **not** presuppose that differently ordered derivatives are equal. Equality of their values is a later theorem under these regularity hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-metrics-on-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$. A von Neumann natural is the set of its\npredecessors, $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), so it can be\nused directly as an index set. Define\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^n := \\{\\, x : x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\},$$\n\nand write $x_k$ for $x(k)$, $k < n$. Two elements of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ are equal\nexactly when they agree at every $k < n$, functions being equal when they have\nthe same values. For $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ put\n\n$$d_1(x,y) := \\sum_{k<n} |x_k - y_k|, \\qquad d_2(x,y) := \\sqrt{\\ \\sum_{k<n} (x_k - y_k)^2\\ }, \\qquad d_\\infty(x,y) := \\max\\{\\, |x_k - y_k| : k < n \\,\\}.$$\n\nAll three are well defined: the finite sums are those of [[def-finite-sum]]; the\nsum of squares is nonnegative ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]], [[lem-of-square-positive]])\nso it has a unique nonnegative square root ([[thm-of-square-roots]]); and\n$\\{|x_k - y_k| : k < n\\}$ is a nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$, because\n$n \\ge 1$, so it has a maximum ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]).\n\n**Then $d_1$, $d_2$ and $d_\\infty$ are metrics on $\\mathbb{R}^n$**\n([[def-metric-space]]).\n\n**Why $n \\ge 1$.** For $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^0$ has exactly one element,\nthe empty function, and $d_1$ and $d_2$ are the empty sum $0$ and its root; but\n$d_\\infty$ would be the maximum of the empty set, which does not exist. The\nhypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is therefore not decoration, and it is carried by every\nstatement about $d_\\infty$ in this library.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-metric-open-set-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), with open and closed sets\nas in [[def-metric-topology]] and balls as in [[def-metric-ball]]. Then:\n\n1. **Balls are open.** $B(x,r)$ is open, for every $x \\in X$ and every $r > 0$.\n2. **Arbitrary unions.** If $\\mathcal{U}$ is any collection of open subsets of\n   $X$, then $\\bigcup \\mathcal{U}$ is open.\n3. **Finite intersections.** If $n \\ge 1$ and $U_0, \\dots, U_{n-1}$ are open,\n   then $U_0 \\cap \\dots \\cap U_{n-1}$ is open.\n4. **Closed balls are closed.** $\\bar B(x,r)$ is closed, for every $x \\in X$ and\n   every $r > 0$.\n\nTogether with the fact that $\\emptyset$ and $X$ are open, recorded already in\n[[def-metric-topology]], claims 2 and 3 say that $\\mathcal{T}_d$ has exactly the\nclosure properties that the word *topology* names.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
