        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-em-smooth-newtonian-upgrade",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $\\varepsilon_0>0$ be any scalar parameter and let $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R)$ and let\n\n$$Nf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{f(y)}{4\\pi|x-y|}\\,dy .$$\n\nThen $Nf\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$, $-\\Delta Nf=f$ pointwise, and for every multi-index $\\alpha$ the spatial derivative satisfies $D^\\alpha Nf=N(D^\\alpha f)$. In particular, for smooth compactly supported $\\rho$ the field $E=-\\varepsilon_0^{-1}\\nabla N\\rho$ is smooth. The statement is about the explicit integral transform of compactly supported smooth data and does not assume smoothness of the kernel at the pole; the distributional identity $-\\Delta Nf=f$ is the one supplied by the Newtonian-potential theorem ([[thm-newtonian-potential-solves-poisson-distributionally]]), upgraded here from a distributional to a pointwise identity for smooth data.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-newtonian-potential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and $n\\ge2$. For a measurable compactly supported\n$f$, define\n$$Nf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\\Phi(x-y)f(y)\\,dy$$\nat each $x$ where the integral is absolutely finite. Whenever this integral\nis finite almost everywhere, $Nf$ also denotes its almost-everywhere class.\nThe following items establish everywhere convergence for bounded compact data\nand almost-everywhere convergence for compact $L^1$ data; the definition itself\nmakes no unconditional convergence claim.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-decay-of-the-newtonian-potential-of-compactly-supported-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and let $n\\ge2$. Fix $R>0$, and let\n$f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ be Lebesgue measurable, integrable, and compactly\nsupported, with $\\|f\\|_1:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}|f(y)|\\,dy<\\infty$, and zero outside the open\nEuclidean ball $B_R(0)$. Put\n$M:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f(y)\\,dy$ and let $\\omega_{n-1}$ be the sphere-area\nnormalization in the definition of $\\Phi$.\n\nFor every $x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $r:=|x|>2R$, the Newtonian integral\n$Nf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\\Phi(x-y)f(y)\\,dy$ is absolutely finite. If $n\\ge3$,\nthen\n$$\n\\left|Nf(x)-\\frac{M r^{2-n}}{(n-2)\\omega_{n-1}}\\right| \\le \\frac{2^{n-1}R}{\\omega_{n-1}}\\|f\\|_1 r^{1-n}.\n$$\nIf $n=2$, then\n$$\n\\left|Nf(x)+\\frac{M}{2\\pi}\\log r\\right| \\le \\frac{R}{\\pi}\\|f\\|_1 r^{-1}.\n$$\nThe constants are independent of the direction of $x$. In particular, when\n$M=0$ these are the respective improved orders\n$Nf(x)=O(r^{1-n})$ for $n\\ge3$ and $Nf(x)=O(r^{-1})$ for $n=2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-em-newtonian-far-field-gradient",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), the convention of the Lebesgue interface used below ([[def-countable-choice]]).\nLet $n\\ge3$, let $\\Phi$ be the fundamental solution of $-\\Delta$ on $\\mathbb R^n$ normalised by $-\\Delta T_\\Phi=\\delta_0$ ([[def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign]]), so that for $z\\ne0$\n$$\\Phi(z)=\\frac{|z|^{2-n}}{(n-2)\\omega_{n-1}},\\qquad \\nabla\\Phi(z)=-\\frac{z}{\\omega_{n-1}|z|^n},\\qquad |\\nabla\\Phi(z)|=\\frac{1}{\\omega_{n-1}|z|^{n-1}},$$\nlet $f\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n)$ be real, measurable and zero $\\lambda_n$-almost everywhere outside a closed ball $\\overline B_R(0)=\\{x:|x|\\le R\\}$ of finite radius $R>0$ ([[def-metric-ball]]), and let\n$$Nf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\\Phi(x-y)f(y)\\,d\\lambda_n(y)$$\nbe the Newtonian potential ([[def-newtonian-potential]]). Then:\n\n(a) [Representation and differentiability off the far field] for every $x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $|x|>2R$ the integral $\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\\nabla\\Phi(x-y)f(y)\\,d\\lambda_n(y)$ is absolutely convergent, the function $Nf$ is differentiable at $x$ with\n$$\\nabla(Nf)(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\\nabla\\Phi(x-y)f(y)\\,d\\lambda_n(y),$$\nand $x\\mapsto\\nabla(Nf)(x)$ is continuous on the open set $\\{x:|x|>2R\\}$;\n\n(b) [Decay bound] for every $x$ with $|x|>2R$,\n$$|\\nabla(Nf)(x)|\\le\\frac{2^{n-1}\\|f\\|_1}{\\omega_{n-1}|x|^{n-1}},\\qquad \\|f\\|_1:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}|f|\\,d\\lambda_n;$$\n\n(c) [Scope] the hypothesis is compact support together with $L^1$-integrability; no boundedness of $f$ is assumed, and nothing is asserted at points with $|x|\\le2R$ (where the kernel singularity may meet the support of $f$).",
          "uses": [
