          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-the-divergence-and-curl-of-a-cross-product",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open and let $F,G:U\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be $C^1$. Then $F\\times G$ is $C^1$ on $U$ and\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}(F\\times G)=\\langle\\operatorname{curl}F,G\\rangle-\\langle F,\\operatorname{curl}G\\rangle,$$\n\n$$\\operatorname{curl}(F\\times G)=(\\operatorname{div}G)F-(\\operatorname{div}F)G+DF\\,G-DG\\,F.$$\n\nHere $DF\\,G$ denotes the map $U\\to\\mathbb R^3$ whose $i$th coordinate at $p$ is $\\sum_{j<3}\\partial_jF_i(p)\\,G_j(p)$, that is, the Jacobian matrix of $F$ at $p$ applied to the vector $G(p)$, and $DG\\,F$ is defined the same way with the roles of $F$ and $G$ exchanged. The operators are those of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], the cross product is that of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], the inner product that of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and the Jacobian matrix that of [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]].",
          "uses": [
