and the boundary-orientation conventions cited there); composing each arc with the $C^2$ map $r(\\cdot,t)$ gives a finite concatenation of $C^1$ arcs with matching one-sided endpoints and equal initial and terminal points, that is, a closed piecewise $C^1$ path in the sense of [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]]. Its trace is $C(t)\\subseteq S$, and $E+v\\times B$ is continuous on a neighbourhood of $S$, hence on the trace; the line integral of (c) is therefore defined and independent of the admissible partition of the path. The integral is unchanged by the chosen $C^2$ extension of $r$, because the path and the integrand depend only on the values of $r$ and $v$ on $D\\times I$ and on the neighbourhood of $S$ recorded in the data; it depends on the orientation of $D$, as recorded in (b).\n\n*The embedded sheet and its boundary.* Because $r(\\cdot,t)$ is injective and continuous on the compact set $D$, each slice $S(t)=r(D,t)$ is an embedded sheet homeomorphic to $D$, and $r(\\partial D,t)=\\partial S(t)$ is its topological boundary; the parametrized path $\\gamma_t$ therefore parametrizes the positively oriented boundary of $S(t)$ induced by the positive orientation of $D$, with the orientation fixed by the boundary chain as in (b). Rank failures on the boundary of the parameter region (seams) remain permitted, since $r_u\\times r_v\\ne0$ is required only on the interior.\n\n*No physical assertion.* The definition introduces geometric data, a flux integral and an emf line integral, with the stated regularity, orientation and unit conventions. It asserts no field equations, no relation between $\\mathrm{emf}$ and $\\Phi_B'$, no conservation law, and no statement about singular sources; the transport identity is proved separately under its own hypotheses.",
