          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-em-moving-flux",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]). In the setting of [[def-em-moving-patch]]: $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ is a finite elementary Green region with positive boundary chain $\\partial D$, $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is an interval, $r:D\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ extends to a $C^2$ map on a neighbourhood of $D\\times I$ with $r_u\\times r_v\\ne0$ on $D^\\circ\\times I^\\circ$, $(W,v)$ is a $C^1$ velocity field with $v(r(p,t),t)=r_t(p,t)$, and $B$ is a $C^1$ field on an open set containing the swept set $S=\\bigcup_{t\\in I}r(D\\times\\{t\\})$, with flux\n\n$$\\Phi(t)=\\int_D\\bigl\\langle B(r(p,t),t),\\,r_u(p,t)\\times r_v(p,t)\\bigr\\rangle\\,dp .$$\n\nIf $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ on its domain, then $\\Phi$ is differentiable on $I$, with relative one-sided derivatives at included endpoints, and for every $t\\in I$ whose slice is regular, that is $r_u(p,t)\\times r_v(p,t)\\ne0$ for all $p\\in D^\\circ$,\n\n$$\\Phi'(t)=\\int_{S(t)}\\partial_tB\\cdot n\\,dS-\\oint_{\\partial S(t)}(v\\times B)\\cdot d\\mathbf r .$$\n\nBy the standing hypothesis on $D^\\circ\\times I^\\circ$ the slice is regular at every interior time $t\\in I^\\circ$, so for open $I$ the identity holds at every $t\\in I$; at an included endpoint of a nondegenerate interval it is asserted exactly when the endpoint slice is regular as well, and no assertion is made when it is not. Here $S(t)=r(D\\times\\{t\\})$ carries the orientation induced by the parametrization, $n$ is the induced unit normal ([[def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch]]), and the boundary integral is taken around the induced boundary chain $\\varphi(\\partial D)$ of [[def-the-induced-boundary-chain-of-a-c2-surface-patch]] with the positive orientation of [[def-positive-orientation-for-elementary-region-boundaries]] and the line integrals of [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]]. Without the hypothesis $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ the additional term\n\n$$\\int_{S(t)}(v\\cdot n)\\,\\operatorname{div}B\\,dS$$\n\nappears on the right-hand side. The identity is local in $t$ and is a statement about the parametrized mathematical object; no electromagnetic interpretation is asserted.",
