          "quote": "Let the laboratory region $\\Omega\\times I$ and its inertial chart, the field objects $E,B$ and the units be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]], with $c>0$; here $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ is a nonempty open set and $I$ is a nonempty open interval.\n\n(a) [Moving material circuit] A **moving material circuit** on the laboratory region is the data $(D,r,v)$ specified as follows. $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ is a compact finite elementary Green region with its positive boundary chain ([[def-em-moving-patch]]); $r:D\\times I\\to\\Omega$ extends to a $C^2$ map on a neighbourhood of $D\\times I$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) with $r_u\\times r_v\\ne0$ on the interior of $D$ and $r(\\cdot,t)$ injective on $D$ for every $t\\in I$ (embedded material sheet); and $v$ is a $C^1$ vector field on a neighbourhood of the swept set $S=\\bigcup_{t\\in I}r(D\\times\\{t\\})$ in $\\Omega\\times I$ satisfying the single-valued condition $v(r(u,w,t),t)=\\partial_tr(u,w,t)$ for all $(u,w)\\in D$ and all $t\\in I$. In the notation of [[def-em-moving-patch]] the map $r$ is the swept moving patch and $v$ is its single-valued velocity; material points of the circuit are the images of the parameter points $(u,w)$.\n\n(b) [Surface, boundary and flux] For $t\\in I$ put $S(t):=r(D,t)$ and let\n$$\\gamma_t:=r(\\cdot,t)|_{\\partial D}$$\nbe the positive boundary chain of $D$ composed with $r(\\cdot,t)$; its trace is the set $C(t):=r(\\partial D,t)$, and $C(t)$ carries the boundary orientation induced by the positive orientation of $D$. For $B$ of class $C^1$ on a neighbourhood of the swept set, the **magnetic flux** of the circuit at time $t$ is the parametrized flux\n$$\\Phi_B(t):=\\int_D\\bigl\\langle B(r(u,w,t),t),\\,r_u(u,w,t)\\times r_v(u,w,t)\\bigr\\rangle\\,du\\,dw$$\nof [[def-em-moving-patch]]; its unit is the weber, $\\mathrm{Wb}=\\mathrm{T}\\,\\mathrm{m}^2$.\n\n(c) [Electromotive force] For $E,B$ continuous on a neighbourhood of the swept set, the **electromotive force** (emf) of the circuit at time $t$ is the vector line integral over the oriented closed path $\\gamma_t$\n$$\\mathrm{emf}(t):=\\oint_{\\gamma_t}\\bigl\\langle E(\\cdot,t)+v(\\cdot,t)\\times B(\\cdot,t),\\,d\\mathbf r\\bigr\\rangle$$\nin the convention of [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]], with the cross product of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]; its unit is the volt, $1\\,\\mathrm V=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m^2\\,\\mathrm s^{-3}\\mathrm A^{-1}$. Under the framework's Lorentz coupling the integrand is the force per unit charge on a charge moving with the conductor material; the definition itself only names the line integral.\n\n(d) [Scope and exclusions] The fields are assumed of the stated regularity on a neighbourhood of the swept set: circuits passing through a point charge, a curve or surface singularity, or any place where the fields fail to be of that regularity are excluded (no singularity crossings). When the emf is interpreted through the framework's relativistic Lorentz coupling, material speeds are required to be subluminal, $|v|<c$. The definition asserts no conservation law and no relation between $\\mathrm{emf}$ and $\\Phi_B'$; in particular the flux concerns the single field $B$ and the emf the pair $(E,B)$, and neither object is claimed independent of the chosen parametrization.\n\n### Well-definedness and conventions\n\n*The flux is well defined.* With $B$ and $r$ of the stated regularity, the integrand of (b) is continuous on the compact Jordan measurable set $D$, so the Riemann integral exists and coincides with the flux of the moving patch $(D,r,v)$ in [[def-em-moving-patch]]; the value is independent of any $C^2$ extension of $r$ by the conventions recorded there.\n\n*The boundary path is a closed piecewise $C^1$ path.* The positive boundary chain of a finite elementary Green region is a finite concatenation of $C^1$ arcs ([[def-em-moving-patch]] and the boundary-orientation conventions cited there); composing each arc with the $C^2$ map $r(\\cdot,t)$ gives a finite concatenation of $C^1$ arcs with matching one-sided endpoints and equal initial and terminal points, that is, a closed piecewise $C^1$ path in the sense of [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]]. Its trace is $C(t)\\subseteq S$, and $E+v\\times B$ is continuous on a neighbourhood of $S$, hence on the trace; the line integral of (c) is therefore defined and independent of the admissible partition of the path. The integral is unchanged by the chosen $C^2$ extension of $r$, because the path and the integrand depend only on the values of $r$ and $v$ on $D\\times I$ and on the neighbourhood of $S$ recorded in the data; it depends on the orientation of $D$, as recorded in (b).\n\n*The embedded sheet and its boundary.* Because $r(\\cdot,t)$ is injective and continuous on the compact set $D$, each slice $S(t)=r(D,t)$ is an embedded sheet homeomorphic to $D$, and $r(\\partial D,t)=\\partial S(t)$ is its topological boundary; the parametrized path $\\gamma_t$ therefore parametrizes the positively oriented boundary of $S(t)$ induced by the positive orientation of $D$, with the orientation fixed by the boundary chain as in (b). Rank failures on the boundary of the parameter region (seams) remain permitted, since $r_u\\times r_v\\ne0$ is required only on the interior.\n\n*No physical assertion.* The definition introduces geometric data, a flux integral and an emf line integral, with the stated regularity, orientation and unit conventions. It asserts no field equations, no relation between $\\mathrm{emf}$ and $\\Phi_B'$, no conservation law, and no statement about singular sources; the transport identity is proved separately under its own hypotheses.",
