          "source": "lem-em-newtonian-linkage-symmetry",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $N$ be the Newtonian potential transform of [[def-newtonian-potential]] in dimension $n=3$: for a finite-valued function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ whose support $\\operatorname{supp}f=\\overline{\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:f(x)\\ne0\\}}$ is compact ([[def-support-and-compactly-supported-riemann-integral-in-rn]]),\n$$Nf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\Phi(x-y)f(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)$$\nat each $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ where this integral is absolutely finite, where $\\Phi$ is the normalized kernel of [[def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign]] with its value at the pole assigned $0$, so that $\\Phi(z)=1/(4\\pi|z|)$ for $z\\ne0$; write also $g(0):=0$ and $g(z):=|z|^{-1}$ for $z\\ne0$, so that $\\Phi=g/(4\\pi)$ pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$. Let $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ be the Euclidean inner product of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], and let $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ be the set of maps each of whose three components is smooth ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) with compact support in the above sense. For $u,v\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ write $Nu:=(Nu_1,Nu_2,Nu_3)$ componentwise, so that the linkage pairing is the map\n$$B(u,v):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\langle u(x),Nv(x)\\rangle\\,d\\lambda_3(x).$$\nThen all three integrals\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\langle u(x),Nv(x)\\rangle\\,d\\lambda_3(x),\\qquad\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\langle Nu(x),v(x)\\rangle\\,d\\lambda_3(x),\\qquad\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\langle u(x),v(y)\\rangle}{|x-y|}\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(x)$$\nconverge absolutely ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]]) and have the same value; therefore $B$ is well defined, real-valued, symmetric, $B(u,v)=B(v,u)$, and bilinear over $\\mathbb R$ in each argument. In particular the common value of the three integrals is unchanged by exchanging the two density fields $u$ and $v$.",
