          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "post-em-circuit-approximation",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as a modelling postulate of this framework the following reduced circuit description, to be used only within its declared regime. Let the laboratory region, its chart and units and the compact smooth current class be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-si-conventions]] and [[def-em-steady-current-regime]], and let the material response be one of the branches of [[post-em-material-closure]].\n\n(a) [Circuit data] Fix a finite set of branches $\\alpha=1,\\dots,n$ (and capacitive branches $\\beta=1,\\dots,m$) and a time interval $J$. The model carries branch currents $I_\\alpha:J\\to\\mathbb R$, branch charges $Q_\\beta:J\\to\\mathbb R$, terminal potentials $V_\\alpha:J\\to\\mathbb R$, and constant matrices $L\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$, $C\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times m}$ and $R\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$, with $L$ and $C$ symmetric, $C$ positive definite and $R$ symmetric positive semidefinite. The matrices, the terminal geometry and the choice of independent loops are fixed data of the model; they are declared, not derived here, and the smooth compact-current realization of the inductive couplings and its reciprocity are analysed separately on this page.\n\n(b) [Branch relations and network laws] In the declared regime the inductive branches obey the Faraday branch relation $V(t)=L\\dot I(t)$, i.e. each terminal potential is the rate of change of the branch flux linkage $LI$; the capacitive branches obey $V=C^{-1}Q$ (in the scalar case $V_\\beta=Q_\\beta/C_{\\beta\\beta}$); the resistive branches obey $V=RI$, the circuit form of the Ohmic relation of [[post-em-material-closure]]; and the network laws are Kirchhoff's current law at each node and loop voltage balance around each independent loop. The circuit-level bookkeeping quantities of the model are the flux linkage $LI$ and the stored energies $\\tfrac12I^{\\mathsf T}LI$ and $\\tfrac12Q^{\\mathsf T}C^{-1}Q$.\n\n(c) [Quasistatic regime declaration] The description is confined to configurations whose largest geometric extent $\\ell$ and shortest time scale $T$ of interest satisfy the quasistatic smallness condition $\\ell/(cT)\\ll1$, with negligible radiation and negligible leakage and with specified conductor geometry and ideal terminals. The inequality is a declared regime of validity of the model; no error bound for an arbitrary full Maxwell solution follows from it alone, and the model does not assert that every asymptotically small configuration is described to any particular accuracy.\n\n(d) [Status, units and scope] This is an adopted reduced model, not a deduction from the vacuum Maxwell equations; the matrices $L,C,R$ are model data, no microscopic constitutive law beyond the adopted branch of [[post-em-material-closure]] is asserted, and no radiation, distributed-element, nonlinear, hysteretic, leakage or proximity effect is included. SI units consistent with [[def-em-si-conventions]] are used: $V$ in volts, $I$ in amperes, $Q$ in coulombs, $t$ in seconds, $L$ in henries ($\\mathrm H=\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm s\\,\\mathrm A^{-1}$), $C$ in farads ($\\mathrm F=\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm V^{-1}$) and $R$ in ohms ($\\Omega=\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm A^{-1}$). Where conductor matter moves, material speeds are subluminal, $|v|<c$, when the relativistic force interpretation is used.",
