          "claim": "Fix the $C^1$ extension of $I$ to an open interval $U\\supseteq J$, and define $W(y):=\\frac12\\langle y,Ly\\rangle=\\frac12\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta}L_{\\alpha\\beta}y_\\alpha y_\\beta$ for $y\\in\\mathbb R^n$, so that $t\\mapsto G(t):=W(I(t))=\\frac12\\langle I(t),LI(t)\\rangle$ is the bookkeeping-energy function of the Statement. For each pair $(\\alpha,\\beta)$ the product $t\\mapsto I_\\alpha(t)I_\\beta(t)$ is differentiable on $U$ with derivative $\\dot I_\\alpha I_\\beta+I_\\alpha\\dot I_\\beta$ by [F3], and $G$ is a finite linear combination of these products; applying the sum and scalar rules of [F3] repeatedly therefore gives, for every $t\\in J$, $$G'(t)=\\frac12\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta}L_{\\alpha\\beta}\\bigl(\\dot I_\\alpha(t)I_\\beta(t)+I_\\alpha(t)\\dot I_\\beta(t)\\bigr)=\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta}L_{\\alpha\\beta}\\dot I_\\alpha(t)I_\\beta(t)=\\langle L\\dot I(t),I(t)\\rangle=\\langle V(t),I(t)\\rangle ,$$ where the second equality relabels $\\alpha\\leftrightarrow\\beta$ in one half of the sum and uses the symmetry $L_{\\alpha\\beta}=L_{\\beta\\alpha}$, and the last two use [F10] and $V=L\\dot I$; the derivative $G'$ is continuous on $J$, being a finite sum of products of continuous functions.",
