          "claim": "Let $u:=\\min\\{t_0,t_1\\}$ and $v:=\\max\\{t_0,t_1\\}$; then $[u,v]\\subseteq J$, since $J$ is order-convex by [F2] and contains both $u$ and $v$. If $u<v$, then $G$ is differentiable at every point of $[u,v]$ as a function on $[u,v]$ (interior points lie in the open set $U$, and at $u,v$ the one-sided derivatives exist by restriction of the extension), while $G'$ is continuous on $[u,v]$ by step 1.1 and hence integrable there by [F4]; [F5] gives $\\int_u^vG'=G(v)-G(u)$, that is $\\int_u^v\\langle V,I\\rangle=G(v)-G(u)$ by step 1.1. If $u=v$ the same display is $0=G(v)-G(u)$, which holds identically and is the $u=v$ reading of [F6]. If $t_0>t_1$, applying the already-proved case to the pair $(t_1,t_0)$ and the sign clause of [F6] gives $\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\\langle V,I\\rangle=-\\int_{t_1}^{t_0}\\langle V,I\\rangle=-(G(t_0)-G(t_1))=G(t_1)-G(t_0)$. In every case $\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\\langle V(t),I(t)\\rangle\\,dt=\\frac12\\langle I(t_1),LI(t_1)\\rangle-\\frac12\\langle I(t_0),LI(t_0)\\rangle$, which is the identity of (a); for $n=1$ the quadratic form is $\\frac12LI^2$ by [F10].",
