          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice for the cited unit-Dirichlet solvability and trace interfaces, and its Countable Choice consequence for surface and integral interfaces ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $(\\Omega;D_1,\\dots,D_m;\\Omega_v)$ be the conductor geometry of [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]], additionally with $\\Omega$ a bounded $C^\\infty$ ambient domain and $\\Omega_v$ connected and with the **outer boundary $\\partial\\Omega$ grounded**: the data on $\\partial\\Omega$ are the constant Dirichlet datum $0$; let $\\rho_v\\equiv0$ on $\\Omega_v$ (the pure capacitor configuration: no volume source), and let the framework constants be those of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. For each $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ let $\\varphi_j\\in C^\\infty(\\overline{\\Omega_v})$ be the unit-potential solution of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-existence]](c) with the smooth data\n$$\\varphi_j|_{\\partial D_k}=\\delta_{jk},\\qquad \\varphi_j|_{\\partial\\Omega}=0,$$\nso that $-\\Delta\\varphi_j=0$ on $\\Omega_v$ with the Laplacian of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], and let $\\nu$ be the outward normal of $\\Omega_v$ (so on $\\partial D_j$ the normal $\\nu$ points into the conductor). Define the **capacitance matrix** by\n$$C_{jk}:=\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\partial D_j}\\frac{\\partial\\varphi_k}{\\partial\\nu}\\,dS,\\qquad j,k=1,\\dots,m,$$\nthe flux integrals of continuous functions over the compact embedded hypersurfaces $\\partial D_j$ (normal derivative of [[def-classical-normal-derivative]]) ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]], [[lem-em-vacuum-region-of-conductors-is-a-bounded-smooth-domain]]). Then:\n(a) [Linearity and matrix form] for every $V=(V_1,\\dots,V_m)\\in\\mathbb R^m$ the unique equilibrium potential with conductor potentials $V$ and grounded outer boundary is $\\varphi^V=\\sum_{j=1}^mV_j\\varphi_j$, and the conductor charges of [[pthm-em-conductor-surface-charge]](d) are\n$$Q_j(V)=\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\partial D_j}\\frac{\\partial\\varphi^V}{\\partial\\nu}\\,dS=\\sum_{k=1}^mC_{jk}V_k,\\qquad\\text{i.e.}\\qquad Q=CV.$$\n(b) [Reciprocity] $C$ is symmetric: $C_{jk}=C_{kj}$ for all $j,k$; the symmetry is Green's symmetric pairing of the harmonic potentials $\\varphi_j,\\varphi_k$.\n(c) [Energy identity] the field energy of the configuration is\n$$U(V)=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\int_{\\Omega_v}|\\nabla\\varphi^V|^2\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac12V^{T}CV=\\frac12\\sum_{j=1}^mV_jQ_j(V),$$\nand $U(V)>0$ for every $V\\ne0$: $C$ is symmetric positive definite. In particular $U(V)\\ge0$, with equality exactly for $V=0$, so every nonzero conductor-potential data set stores positive field energy.\n(d) [Two-conductor reading] with only conductor $1$ driven and every other conductor grounded, $V=te_1$: $Q_1=C_{11}t$, $Q_j=C_{j1}t$ for $j\\ne1$, and $U=\\tfrac12C_{11}t^2=\\tfrac12Q_1V_1=Q_1^2/(2C_{11})$; the **capacitance of the arrangement** at conductor $1$ with respect to the grounded reference is the positive geometric number $C_{11}>0$, with $C_{11}=Q_1/V_1$ when $t=V_1\\ne0$, and the energy identities $U=\\tfrac12QV=Q^2/(2C)=\\tfrac12CV^2$ hold with $C=C_{11}$, $Q=Q_1$, $V=V_1$. By (c) the positivity holds for every diagonal entry, $C_{jj}>0$.\n(e) [Assembly reading] use the family $V_j(s)=sV_j$, $\\varphi_s=s\\varphi^V$, $Q_j(s)=sQ_j(V)$ for $0\\le s\\le1$, with fixed geometry and grounded reference. Prescribe the quasistatic external charge-transfer work by $dW_{\\rm ext}=\\sum_jV_j(s)dQ_j(s)$. Then $W_{\\rm ext}=\\int_0^1s\\,ds\\sum_jV_jQ_j(V)=U$. The potentials vary during assembly; only the final potentials and geometry are fixed. This derives the sheet-charge accounting directly and does not apply the volume-source theorem [[pthm-em-electrostatic-field-energy]] to singular sheets. The family is an accounting prescription, not an asserted time-dependent Maxwell/material solution.\n(f) [Scope] the theorem assumes: the compact separated conductor geometry, the additional bounded smooth ambient domain, and connected vacuum region of the model, the grounded outer boundary $\\partial\\Omega$, zero volume source, and the existence and uniqueness of the unit-potential Dirichlet solutions ([[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-existence]](c), [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-uniqueness]](a)). It asserts no solvability for arbitrary boundary data (the existence theorem is used only for the constant data above), no prescribed-total-charge problem, no dielectric or material response, no finite-conductivity or transient charging dynamics, no radiation, and no measurement; the matrix $C$ is a geometric object of the ideal electrostatic model, and the ideal parallel-plate and similar formulas are statements about this model only.",
