          "source": "pthm-em-inductor-energy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the lumped circuit model of [[post-em-circuit-approximation]] with the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]) for clause (b), where it is inherited from the linkage suppliers.\n\n(a) [Terminal-energy bookkeeping] Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval ([[def-interval]]), let $t_0,t_1\\in J$, let $L\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ be constant and symmetric, let $I:J\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be of class $C^1$ in the following sense: $I$ extends to a $C^1$ map on an open interval containing $J$, so that the derivatives used below are those of the extension (one-sided at included endpoints of $J$, and not intrinsic to a value restriction when $J$ is a singleton), and let $V(t):=L\\dot I(t)$ be the terminal-voltage vector of the inductive branches as adopted in [[post-em-circuit-approximation]] (the Faraday branch: the terminal voltage of a branch is the rate of change of its flux linkage $\\Phi=LI$). Then\n$$\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\\langle V(t),I(t)\\rangle\\,dt=\\frac12\\langle I(t_1),LI(t_1)\\rangle-\\frac12\\langle I(t_0),LI(t_0)\\rangle ,$$\nand for $n=1$ with $L>0$ this reads $\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}V I\\,dt=\\frac12L\\bigl(I(t_1)^2-I(t_0)^2\\bigr)$: the accumulated terminal energy equals the change of the inductor bookkeeping energy $\\frac12LI^2$. The identity is exact for every such $C^1$ current and fixed geometry; it uses only symmetry of $L$, the product rule and the fundamental theorem of calculus. For a changing geometry the mechanical work done on the circuit is an additional term and is not covered here.\n\n(b) [Identification with the frozen field energy] Suppose in addition that the currents are realized by fixed compact smooth conserved profiles $j_1,\\dots,j_n$ of the class of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](b), with instantaneous source $J(t):=\\sum_\\alpha I_\\alpha(t)j_\\alpha$, and that the inductance matrix is the linkage matrix of those profiles in the sense of [[pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity]]: $L_{\\alpha\\beta}=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\iint\\frac{\\langle j_\\alpha(x),j_\\beta(y)\\rangle}{|x-y|}\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(x)$. Then for every $t\\in J$\n$$\\frac12\\langle I(t),LI(t)\\rangle=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\langle J(t),A_{J(t)}\\rangle\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac1{2\\mu_0}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|B_{J(t)}|^2\\,d\\lambda_3\\ \\ge0,$$\nwhere $A_{J(t)}$ is the Coulomb-gauge Newtonian vector potential and $B_{J(t)}=\\operatorname{curl}A_{J(t)}$ of that theorem: the right-hand side is the magnetic field energy of the frozen instantaneous configuration, and the accumulated terminal energy of (a) between $t_0$ and $t_1$ is the change of this field energy. The relation $V=L\\dot I$ is the explicitly adopted terminal law of [[post-em-circuit-approximation]]; the identification of the model's inductance matrix with the smooth-profile linkage matrix is part of the adopted model of [[post-em-circuit-approximation]].\n\n(c) [Scope] no radiation, distributed-element, material-loss, moving-geometry or thin-wire self-inductance claim is made; the frozen-configuration energy is not asserted to be the energy of a time-dependent full Maxwell solution.",
